et a  topic for your empirical thesis is to  see if you can find the study that you  can modify or replicate an example a  study used population X did experiment  that and presented the results in a  certain way now you simply choose a  different population and do everything  else the same the advantage is you can  stick to the research design and the  methods used in the existing study you  then have a guideline from the beginning  that you can follow  important there are different  requirements depending on the type of  scientific work for a master's thesis or  a dissertation you should find your own  research Gap in the literature and  address it on your own for this you can  ask yourself the following question what  is the added value of your topic to the  literature think about how your research  can address a topic that has not been  studied before to come up with topics  that have not been explored yet the  following list can help you  investigation of an unusual place for  example Rural America investigating an  unusual group of participants taking a  perspective that may not be expected and  reversing the expectation for example  why marriages work rather than why they  don't  providing novel means of collecting data  for example capturing sounds  presenting results in unusual ways for  example charts showing Geographic  locations investigating a current issue  for example the high inflation  but how do you get from the research  topic to the specific research problem  to get to the heart of your topic you  can try to Define your work more  precisely with the following questions  thus the subject need to be restricted  locally thus the topic need to be  limited in time which contents or terms  of the topic can be specified and  defined more precisely does your topic  indicate the method used or the data  being studied and thus the topic  indicate the goal of your work let's  walk through this with an example let's  assume your research topic is initially  the political discourse in the media now  let's go through the Five Points first  thus the subject need to be restricted  locally the political discourse in the  media we will certainly not look at the  whole world let's say you're interested  in the U.S media  number two does the topic need to be  limited in time the political discourse  in the U.S media but what time let's say  you're interested in the political  discourse in 2020  number three which contents or terms of  the topic can be specified and defined  more precisely political discourse in  the U.S media in 2020 which political  discourse and which U.S media you're  interested in the political discourse on  the Corona pandemic in the U.S  newspapers The Wall Street Journal the  New York Times and USA Today  thus your topic indicate the method used  or the data being studied  well at least we know that the data  comes from the daily newspapers Wall  Street Journal New York Times and USA  Today in 2020 and the last Point number  five thus the topic indicate the goal of  your work your goal is a media critical  comparison this brings us from the broad  topic the political discourse in the  media to the much more specified  research problem the political discourse  on the Corona pandemic in the U.S  newspapers The Wall Street Journal the  New York Times and USA Today in the year  2020 a media critical comparison  with this you have mastered the  transition from the research topic to  the more specific research problem you  have now defined exactly what you want  to study in your thesis  but what about the central research  question the central question is the  overarching question you want to answer  in your thesis  simply put the central question is the  research problem in question form let's  go back to our example with the  political discourse  our specified topic or research problem  here is  to get to your central question you can  now simply  reformulate your research problem into a  question here it is useful to use other  words or synonyms your central question  could therefore be how is the debate on  the Corona pandemic at the political  level in 2020 presented in the U.S  newspapers The Wall Street Journal the  New York Times and USA Today  as shown in this video finding a  research topic and literature review is  an iterative process it is also possible  that you simply start in the literature  research with a certain keyword and then  find your way to a suitable topic with  the help of the literature  note the later you do a literature  research the more difficult you make  your life so it's best to start with a  first literature research as early as  possible and now we continue with the  video on literature research we will  discuss how to approach a systematic  literature research how to prepare the  current state of research how to develop  the theoretical background and finally  how to derive research questions and  hypotheses  thanks for watching and I hope to see  you in the next video
 

